properties.text:
Writers are optimistic. Each one runs against its pinned view and finds out at commit whether another transaction got there first.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
  accTitle: A write-write conflict and its retry
  accDescr {
    Connection A begins a transaction and reads Ada's role as engineer.
    Connection B then commits a change to Ada's role in autocommit.
    A still reads engineer from its pinned view; when A commits its own change to Ada, the commit fails with a retryable write-write conflict.
    A begins again, reads B's committed value, writes, and commits.
  }
  A1["A: begin, read Ada's role: engineer"] --> B1["B: set Ada's role to lead, commit"]
  B1 --> A2["A: read again: engineer, from its pinned view"]
  A2 --> A3["A: set Ada's role to architect, commit"]
  A3 -->|"write-write conflict, retryable"| A4["A: begin again, read lead"]
  A4 --> A5["A: set architect, commit: done"]
```

Connection A begins a transaction and reads Ada's role as engineer. Connection B then commits a change to Ada's role in autocommit. A still reads engineer from its pinned view; when A commits its own change to Ada, the commit fails with a retryable write-write conflict. A begins again, reads B's committed value, writes, and commits.

This Node.js program plays the same sequence on the example graph, with two connections to one in-memory store:

```js run name=conflict title="conflict.cjs"
const { Database } = require("@relate/relate");

const db = Database.open(":memory:");
const a = db.connect();
const b = db.connect();

a.query(`CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)`);

const roleOf = (conn) =>
  conn.query("MATCH (p:Person {name: $name}) RETURN p.role AS role", { name: "Ada" }).rows[0].role;

// A begins; its reads now come from the last commit before begin.
a.begin();
console.log(`A reads: ${roleOf(a)}`);

// B changes the same node in autocommit.
b.query("MATCH (p:Person {name: 'Ada'}) SET p.role = 'lead'");
console.log(`B commits, then reads: ${roleOf(b)}`);
console.log(`A still reads: ${roleOf(a)}`);

// A writes the node B changed; the conflict surfaces at commit.
a.query("MATCH (p:Person {name: 'Ada'}) SET p.role = 'architect'");
try {
  a.commit();
} catch (e) {
  console.log(`A's commit fails: ${e.message} (${e.code}, retryable: ${e.retryable})`);
}

// The failed transaction is gone; the retry starts from the latest commit.
a.begin();
console.log(`A's retry reads: ${roleOf(a)}`);
a.query("MATCH (p:Person {name: 'Ada'}) SET p.role = 'architect'");
a.commit();
console.log(`After the retry: ${roleOf(b)}`);
```

```text
A reads: engineer
B commits, then reads: lead
A still reads: engineer
A's commit fails: write-write conflict (Neo.TransientError.Transaction.Outdated, retryable: true)
A's retry reads: lead
After the retry: architect
```

The engine never retries on its own. The client decides whether to run the transaction again, give up, or compensate, and the `retryable` flag tells it which errors can succeed on a second try. `retryable` is `true` for a write-write conflict (exit 5) and for a `--concurrent-logical-workspace-limit` refusal while other statements hold the shared pool (exit 1, kind `resource`; reachable when statements run concurrently, as under `relate serve`). A one-shot CLI run meets only the first. A syntax error, a locked store or a constraint violation is not retryable. The CLI reports the conflict as `code` `conflict` with exit code 5 and `"retryable": true`, and the Python and Node.js clients put the same flag on the exception.

Conflict detection compares the nodes and relationships each transaction wrote. Two transactions that write different elements both commit, even when each read what the other wrote, so the engine does not detect write skew. Uniqueness and key constraints are the exception: commit also rejects the later of two transactions that introduce the same constrained value, with the same retryable conflict, and on the retry the statement reports the precise constraint violation.

An error in a statement never throws. It comes back in the envelope as `error`, a string that starts with the error's kind (`semantic error`) and its name (`ParameterMissing`), and `run()` turns it into a rejected Promise. A statement that uses `$name` without a value:

```cypher run name=error expect=error
MATCH (p:Person {name: $name})
RETURN p.role AS role
```

```table
error: semantic error: ParameterMissing: Expected parameter(s): name
```

From the page, added to the end of `main.js`:

```js
try {
  await run('MATCH (p:Person {name: $name}) RETURN p.role AS role');
} catch (err) {
  out.textContent = err.message;
}
```

```text
semantic error: ParameterMissing: Expected parameter(s): name
```

The [errors reference](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/errors/#engine-parameter-missing) lists this message with its cause and remedy. The engine's state is unchanged, and the next statement runs as usual. The one failure that does throw is a trap: a crash inside the engine, after which that engine instance can't be used again. `isWasmTrap(err)` from `trap.js` says which it was; the program at the end reports a trap like any other failure.

The engine runs in a module worker. A worker has its own thread, so a long [statement](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#statement) never freezes the page, and only a dedicated worker can open a store in OPFS. Create `worker.js`:

```js title="worker.js"
// worker.js: owns the database. The page sends {id, statement, params}
// and gets back {id, envelope}.
const engine = (file) => new URL(`/relate/${file}`, self.location.origin).href;

const ready = (async () => {
  const relate = await import(/* @vite-ignore */ engine('relate.js'));
  await relate.default();
  return new relate.RelateDb();
})();

self.onmessage = async (event) => {
  const { id, statement, params } = event.data;
  const db = await ready;
  const envelope = JSON.parse(db.execute(statement, params ? JSON.stringify(params) : null));
  self.postMessage({ id, envelope });
};
```

`relate.default()` loads the engine, and `new RelateDb()` opens an in-memory store: it lives in the worker and forgets everything when the worker ends. The handler is set before the engine finishes loading, so no message is lost; each one waits on `ready`. `/* @vite-ignore */` tells Vite to leave the import alone.

The page talks to the worker. Create `main.js`, with a `run()` helper that sends one statement and resolves with its result:

```js title="main.js"
const worker = new Worker(new URL('./worker.js', import.meta.url), { type: 'module' });
const pending = new Map();
let nextId = 0;

worker.onmessage = (event) => {
  const { id, envelope } = event.data;
  const { resolve, reject } = pending.get(id);
  pending.delete(id);
  if (envelope.error) reject(new Error(envelope.error));
  else resolve(envelope);
};

function run(statement, params) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const id = nextId++;
    pending.set(id, { resolve, reject });
    worker.postMessage({ id, statement, params });
  });
}

const out = document.getElementById('out');
run("RETURN 'hello' AS greeting").then(({ rows }) => {
  out.textContent = rows[0].greeting;
});
```

And an `index.html` beside them:

```html title="index.html"
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>Team graph</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <pre id="out"></pre>
    <script type="module" src="/main.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Start Vite's dev server with `npx vite` and open the address it prints. The page shows:

```text
hello
```

Notice that the page has no engine code at all: it posts a statement and gets an [envelope](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#envelope) back, the result as one JSON object.

The worker can open a store that survives the worker, too. `RelateDb.open("opfs://<name>")` opens one in OPFS, the browser's private storage for the site, and creates it when nothing is there yet. It takes the place of a [single-file store](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#single-file-store), which the browser build cannot open. The name is 1 to 64 characters from `A-Z`, `a-z`, `0-9`, `_` and `-`. The call returns a Promise, and only a dedicated worker may make it; on the page's own thread it rejects with:

```text
Error: storage error: OPFS needs a dedicated worker
```

The program at the end of this page opens `opfs://team`.

The finished program opens `opfs://team`, creates the graph only when the store has no people yet, and prints who works on each project. Replace `worker.js` with this version, which opens the OPFS store and reports a trap separately:

```js title="worker.js"
// worker.js: owns the team graph in OPFS. The page sends {id, statement, params}
// and gets back {id, envelope}, or {id, failed, trap} when a call throws.
const engine = (file) => new URL(`/relate/${file}`, self.location.origin).href;

let isWasmTrap = () => false;

const ready = (async () => {
  const [relate, trap] = await Promise.all([
    import(/* @vite-ignore */ engine('relate.js')),
    import(/* @vite-ignore */ engine('trap.js')),
  ]);
  isWasmTrap = trap.isWasmTrap;
  await relate.default();
  return relate.RelateDb.open('opfs://team');
})();

self.onmessage = async (event) => {
  const { id, statement, params } = event.data;
  try {
    const db = await ready;
    const envelope = JSON.parse(db.execute(statement, params ? JSON.stringify(params) : null));
    self.postMessage({ id, envelope });
  } catch (err) {
    self.postMessage({ id, failed: String(err?.message ?? err), trap: isWasmTrap(err) });
  }
};
```

And `main.js`:

```js title="main.js"
// main.js: the page. It never touches the engine; every statement goes to
// the worker and comes back as an envelope.
const TEAM = `
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)
`;

const TEAM_OF = `
MATCH (p:Person)-[w:WORKS_ON]->(:Project {name: $project})
RETURN p.name AS name, w.since AS since
ORDER BY since
`;

const worker = new Worker(new URL('./worker.js', import.meta.url), { type: 'module' });
const pending = new Map();
let nextId = 0;

worker.onmessage = (event) => {
  const { id, envelope, failed } = event.data;
  const { resolve, reject } = pending.get(id);
  pending.delete(id);
  if (failed) reject(new Error(failed));
  else if (envelope.error) reject(new Error(envelope.error));
  else resolve(envelope);
};

function run(statement, params) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const id = nextId++;
    pending.set(id, { resolve, reject });
    worker.postMessage({ id, statement, params });
  });
}

const lines = [];
const print = (...parts) => {
  lines.push(parts.join(' '));
  document.getElementById('out').textContent = lines.join('\n');
};

async function main() {
  const { rows } = await run('MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p) AS n');
  if (rows[0].n === 0) {
    await run(TEAM);
    print('created the team graph');
  } else {
    print('found', rows[0].n, 'people');
  }
  for (const project of ['relate', 'opencypher-tck']) {
    print(project);
    const result = await run(TEAM_OF, { project });
    for (const row of result.rows) print(' ', row.name, 'since', row.since);
  }
}

main().catch((err) => print('relate error:', err.message));
```

Open the page. The first load creates the graph:

```text
created the team graph
relate
  Grace since 2023
  Ada since 2024
opencypher-tck
  Grace since 2022
```

Reload it, and the graph is still there:

```text
found 3 people
relate
  Grace since 2023
  Ada since 2024
opencypher-tck
  Grace since 2022
```

Each statement is durable in OPFS when `execute()` returns. One worker at a time holds a store: while one has `opfs://team` open, another worker of the same site that opens it, read-only or not, gets `storage locked: opfs://team`. A reload ends the old page's worker, which releases the store. `npx vite build` writes the same site to `dist/`, with `relate/` copied in beside it.

A statement that fails does not make the call fail. `relate_query_params()` returns `RELATE_OK` with an error result, `relate_result_is_error()` is `1`, and `relate_result_error_json()` holds the error as JSON. A statement that uses `$name` without a value is rejected before it runs:

```c run name=error
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "relate.h"

static const char *TEAM =
    "CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),"
    "       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),"
    "       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),"
    "       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),"
    "       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),"
    "       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

static const char *ROLE_OF = "MATCH (p:Person {name: $name}) RETURN p.role AS role";

/* Print a caller-owned error string, free it, and return 1. */
static int fail(const char *what, char *err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s failed: %s\n", what, err ? err : "(no detail)");
    relate_free_string(err);
    return 1;
}

/* Copy the string member `name` of a flat JSON object into out, or "" when it is absent. */
static void json_string(const char *json, const char *name, char *out, size_t size) {
    char key[64];
    snprintf(key, sizeof key, "\"%s\":\"", name);
    const char *at = strstr(json, key);
    size_t n = 0;
    if (at) {
        at += strlen(key);
        while (at[n] && at[n] != '"' && n + 1 < size) n++;
        memcpy(out, at, n);
    }
    out[n] = '\0';
}

int main(void) {
    relate_database db = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL;
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open(":memory:", &db, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("open", err);
    if (relate_connect_namespace(db, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("connect", err);
    if (relate_query_params(conn, TEAM, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("query", err);
    relate_destroy_result(r);

    /* No value for $name: the call succeeds, and the result is an error result. */
    if (relate_query_params(conn, ROLE_OF, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("query", err);
    if (relate_result_is_error(r)) {
        const char *json = relate_result_error_json(r);
        char gql_status[16];
        json_string(json, "gql_status", gql_status, sizeof gql_status);
        printf("%s\n", json);
        if (strcmp(gql_status, "42N02") == 0) printf("a parameter is missing\n");
    }
    relate_destroy_result(r);

    relate_params params = relate_params_new();
    if (relate_params_put_string(params, "name", "Noor", strlen("Noor"), &err) != RELATE_OK)
        return fail("put_string", err);
    if (relate_query_params(conn, ROLE_OF, params, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("query", err);
    size_t len = 0;
    const char *role = relate_value_string(relate_value_at(r, 0, 0), &len);
    printf("%.*s\n", (int)len, role);
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    relate_params_free(params);

    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(db);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
{"code":"Neo.ClientError.Statement.ParameterMissing","gql_status":"42N02","stage":"compile","retryable":false,"message":"semantic error: ParameterMissing: Expected parameter(s): name"}
a parameter is missing
maintainer
```

The message says what's missing. The JSON's fields are what code branches on:

| Field        | This error                                   | Meaning                                                                                                                        |
| ------------ | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `gql_status` | `42N02`                                      | The GQLSTATUS code; the other clients report the same code for the same error                                                  |
| `code`       | `Neo.ClientError.Statement.ParameterMissing` | The Neo4j status code for the same error                                                                                       |
| `stage`      | `compile`                                    | Where it was found: `compile` before any work, `runtime` while producing rows, `internal` when it is not the statement's fault |
| `retryable`  | `false`                                      | Whether running it again can succeed; `true` for a write-write conflict, never for a mistake in the statement                  |

The [errors reference](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/errors/#engine-parameter-missing) lists this message with its cause and remedy. With the value bound, the same statement finds Noor's role.

A connection commits each statement on its own until `relate_conn_begin()`. From then on, every statement on that connection is part of one transaction, `relate_conn_commit()` keeps it, and `relate_conn_rollback()` throws it away. This program adds Lin to the team and commits, then deletes every project and rolls back.

```c run name=transaction
#include <stdio.h>
#include "relate.h"

static const char *TEAM =
    "CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),"
    "       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),"
    "       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),"
    "       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),"
    "       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),"
    "       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

/* Print a caller-owned error string, free it, and return 1. */
static int fail(const char *what, char *err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s failed: %s\n", what, err ? err : "(no detail)");
    relate_free_string(err);
    return 1;
}

/* Run a statement whose rows are not needed; 0 when it succeeded. */
static int exec(relate_connection conn, const char *cypher) {
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_query_params(conn, cypher, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("query", err);
    int failed = relate_result_is_error(r);
    if (failed) fprintf(stderr, "statement failed: %s\n", relate_result_error_json(r));
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    return failed;
}

/* The count a one-row, one-column INT result holds. */
static long long count(relate_connection conn, const char *cypher) {
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    int64_t n = -1;
    if (relate_query_params(conn, cypher, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) {
        fail("query", err);
        return -1;
    }
    relate_value_int(relate_value_at(r, 0, 0), &n);
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    return n;
}

int main(void) {
    relate_database db = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL;
    char *err = NULL, *bookmark = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open(":memory:", &db, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("open", err);
    if (relate_connect_namespace(db, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("connect", err);
    if (exec(conn, TEAM)) return 1;

    if (relate_conn_begin(conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("begin", err);
    if (exec(conn, "MATCH (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck'}) "
                   "CREATE (:Person {name: 'Lin', role: 'engineer'})-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2026}]->(tck)")) return 1;
    if (relate_conn_commit(conn, &bookmark, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("commit", err);
    printf("committed: %s\n", bookmark ? "yes" : "no");
    relate_free_string(bookmark);
    printf("people after commit: %lld\n", count(conn, "MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p) AS n"));

    if (relate_conn_begin(conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("begin", err);
    if (exec(conn, "MATCH (p:Project) DETACH DELETE p")) return 1;
    printf("projects inside the transaction: %lld\n", count(conn, "MATCH (p:Project) RETURN count(p) AS n"));
    if (relate_conn_rollback(conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("rollback", err);
    printf("projects after rollback: %lld\n", count(conn, "MATCH (p:Project) RETURN count(p) AS n"));

    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(db);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
committed: yes
people after commit: 4
projects inside the transaction: 0
projects after rollback: 2
```

Inside the transaction, the delete is visible: zero projects. After the rollback, both are back, and Lin, who was committed, stays. The commit hands back a [bookmark](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#bookmark), the commit's place in the store's history as `relate:<n>`, or `NULL` when the transaction wrote nothing. That string is the caller's, so the program frees it with `relate_free_string()`.

Our graph is three people and two projects: who works on what, since when, and who maintains `relate`. One `CREATE` statement writes all of it. The rest of this page uses the same statement, as `TEAM`, on an in-memory store, so each program runs on its own.

```c run name=create
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "relate.h"

static const char *TEAM =
    "CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),"
    "       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),"
    "       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),"
    "       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),"
    "       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),"
    "       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

/* Print a caller-owned error string, free it, and return 1. */
static int fail(const char *what, char *err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s failed: %s\n", what, err ? err : "(no detail)");
    relate_free_string(err);
    return 1;
}

/* One counter from the result's stats, a JSON object such as {"nodes_created":5,...}. */
static long long stat(relate_query_result r, const char *name) {
    char key[64];
    snprintf(key, sizeof key, "\"%s\":", name);
    const char *at = strstr(relate_result_stats(r), key);
    return at ? strtoll(at + strlen(key), NULL, 10) : -1;
}

int main(void) {
    relate_database db = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL;
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open(":memory:", &db, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("open", err);
    if (relate_connect_namespace(db, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("connect", err);
    if (relate_query_params(conn, TEAM, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("query", err);
    printf("nodes created: %lld\n", stat(r, "nodes_created"));
    printf("relationships created: %lld\n", stat(r, "relationships_created"));
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(db);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
nodes created: 5
relationships created: 5
```

A write returns no rows, so we read its counters from `relate_result_stats()`, a JSON object string that belongs to the result. Five nodes and five relationships: the whole team.

Each release attaches a `relate-ffi-<target>.tar.gz` archive (`cypherlite-ffi-<target>.tar.gz` up to and including 0.16.1) per platform, holding `include/relate.h` and the static and shared libraries. Unpack it into a directory, `<dir>` in the command, then compile each program on this page against the header and link the shared library. The rpath lets the program find the library when it runs. On macOS, the release library's install name is the absolute path it was built at, so an rpath finds it only after `install_name_tool` gives it an rpath-relative name, once per unpacked archive:

```bash
install_name_tool -id @rpath/librelate_ffi.dylib <dir>/librelate_ffi.dylib   # macOS only
cc -I<dir>/include version.c -L<dir> -lrelate_ffi -Wl,-rpath,<dir> -o version
./version
```

Each program on this page is a whole file, built with that command. The library has no version constant of its own, so this first one asks the engine which release it is, then prints the version of the C application binary interface (ABI) the header describes:

```c run name=version title="version.c"
#include <stdio.h>
#include "relate.h"

int main(void) {
    relate_database db = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL;
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open(":memory:", &db, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    if (relate_connect_namespace(db, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    if (relate_query_params(conn,
            "CALL dbms.components() YIELD name, versions "
            "WHERE name = 'Relate' RETURN versions[0] AS version",
            NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    size_t len = 0;
    const char *version = relate_value_string(relate_value_at(r, 0, 0), &len);
    printf("%.*s\n", (int)len, version);
    printf("ABI version %d\n", relate_abi_version());
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(db);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
1.0.1
ABI version 2
```

Notice the version: the programs on this page were checked against that release, and their recorded output comes from it.

A [store](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#store) is where relate keeps a graph. `:memory:` is a store that lives in the process and forgets everything when it closes. A path ending in `.rl` is a [single-file store](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#single-file-store), which `relate_database_open()` creates when nothing is there yet.

```c run name=open
#include <stdio.h>
#include "relate.h"

int main(void) {
    relate_database memory = NULL, team = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL;
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open(":memory:", &memory, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    if (relate_connect_namespace(memory, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    if (relate_query_params(conn, "RETURN 'hello' AS greeting", NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    size_t len = 0;
    const char *greeting = relate_value_string(relate_value_at(r, 0, 0), &len);
    printf("%.*s\n", (int)len, greeting);
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(memory);

    if (relate_database_open("./team.rl", &team, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    relate_database_close(team);
    FILE *f = fopen("team.rl", "rb");
    printf("team.rl exists: %s\n", f ? "yes" : "no");
    if (f) fclose(f);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
hello
team.rl exists: yes
```

`relate_database_open()` opens a store, `relate_connect_namespace()` opens a connection on it (`NULL` picks the default namespace), and `relate_query_params()` runs one [statement](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#statement) and hands back its whole result. Each call returns `RELATE_OK` or `RELATE_ERROR`, and each handle is yours to free: the result with `relate_destroy_result()`, then the connection, then the store. Notice that `team.rl` now exists.

`team.c` puts the steps together in one program. It opens `team.rl`, creates the graph only when the store has no people yet, and prints who works on each project. An error anywhere ends it with the error and exit status 1.

```c run name=program title="team.c"
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "relate.h"

static const char *TEAM =
    "CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),"
    "       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),"
    "       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),"
    "       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),"
    "       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),"
    "       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

static const char *TEAM_OF =
    "MATCH (p:Person)-[w:WORKS_ON]->(:Project {name: $project}) "
    "RETURN p.name AS name, w.since AS since ORDER BY since";

/* Print an error and return 1; err is a caller-owned string or NULL. */
static int fail(const char *what, char *err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s failed: %s\n", what, err ? err : "(no detail)");
    relate_free_string(err);
    return 1;
}

/* Run one statement; an error result is reported and freed, and NULL comes back. */
static relate_query_result run(relate_connection conn, const char *cypher, relate_params params) {
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_query_params(conn, cypher, params, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) {
        fail("query", err);
        return NULL;
    }
    if (relate_result_is_error(r)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "relate error: %s\n", relate_result_error_json(r));
        relate_destroy_result(r);
        return NULL;
    }
    return r;
}

static int ensure_team(relate_connection conn) {
    relate_query_result r = run(conn, "MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p) AS n", NULL);
    if (!r) return 1;
    int64_t people = 0;
    relate_value_int(relate_value_at(r, 0, 0), &people);
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    if (people == 0) {
        if (!(r = run(conn, TEAM, NULL))) return 1;
        relate_destroy_result(r);
        printf("created the team graph\n");
    } else {
        printf("found %lld people\n", (long long)people);
    }
    return 0;
}

static int report(relate_connection conn, const char *project) {
    char *err = NULL;
    relate_params params = relate_params_new();
    if (relate_params_put_string(params, "project", project, strlen(project), &err) != RELATE_OK) {
        relate_params_free(params);
        return fail("put_string", err);
    }
    relate_query_result r = run(conn, TEAM_OF, params);
    relate_params_free(params);
    if (!r) return 1;
    printf("%s\n", project);
    for (long long row = 0; row < relate_row_count(r); row++) {
        size_t len = 0;
        int64_t since = 0;
        const char *name = relate_value_string(relate_value_at(r, row, 0), &len);
        relate_value_int(relate_value_at(r, row, 1), &since);
        printf("  %.*s since %lld\n", (int)len, name, (long long)since);
    }
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    return 0;
}

int main(void) {
    relate_database db = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open("./team.rl", &db, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("open", err);
    if (relate_connect_namespace(db, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) {
        relate_database_close(db);
        return fail("connect", err);
    }
    int status = ensure_team(conn) || report(conn, "relate") || report(conn, "opencypher-tck");
    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(db);
    return status;
}
```

```text
created the team graph
relate
  Grace since 2023
  Ada since 2024
opencypher-tck
  Grace since 2022
```

Each statement is durable in `team.rl` when `relate_query_params()` returns. Run it a second time and it prints `found 3 people` instead of creating the graph again, then the same report. If another process has `team.rl` open for writing, the open fails: the program prints `open failed:` and the error JSON, whose message is the fixed `internal error`, since no file path crosses the ABI, and exits with status 1.

To read the file with no chance of changing it, open it with `relate_database_open_read_only()` instead. A write then comes back as an error result with GQLSTATUS `42N18`, and a path with no store behind it is an error instead of a new store.

A statement names each value it needs as `$name`. `relate_params_new()` returns a builder, `relate_params_put_string()` binds one value by name, and `relate_query_params()` takes the builder. The statement text stays the same while the values change, and a value never becomes part of the Cypher.

