(no rows) · indexes added: 2, constraints added: 3
```

Claude Code's sandbox (`/sandbox`, or `sandbox.enabled` in a settings file) lets commands write only to the working directory and temporary directories, and allows only listed domains, a list that starts empty. Install relate before the session: `install.sh` writes to `~/.local/bin`, outside the writable set.

For datasets loaded from GitHub, with usage metrics off:

```json title=".claude/settings.json"
{
  "env": { "DO_NOT_TRACK": "1" },
  "sandbox": {
    "enabled": true,
    "network": { "allowedDomains": ["raw.githubusercontent.com"] }
  }
}
```

Run the engine in a dedicated module worker, `new Worker(url, { type: 'module' })`, unless the graph is in memory and every statement is small.

|                                  | Page thread                                             | Dedicated worker             |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| `new RelateDb()` (in memory)     | Works                                                   | Works                        |
| `RelateDb.open("opfs://<name>")` | Rejects: `storage error: OPFS needs a dedicated worker` | Works                        |
| A long statement                 | Blocks the page until it returns                        | Blocks only the worker       |
| Stopping a runaway statement     | Not possible: there is no statement timeout             | `worker.terminate()` ends it |

`execute()` and `executeScript()` are synchronous: each returns when its statements finish.

In the worker, assign `onmessage` before the engine finishes loading and wait for it inside the handler, so the first message is not lost:

```js
const ready = (async () => {
  const relate = await import(/* @vite-ignore */ engine('relate.js'));
  await relate.default();
  return new relate.RelateDb();
})();

self.onmessage = async (event) => {
  const db = await ready;
  self.postMessage(db.execute(event.data.statement, event.data.params ?? null));
};
```

The browser build has no [transaction](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#transaction) API. Every statement commits on its own when `execute()` returns, and `BEGIN` is a syntax error. Within one statement, all of its writes apply or none do, so writes that must land together go in one statement: one `CREATE` with several patterns, or an `UNWIND` over a list parameter.

`executeScript(script, params_json)` runs several statements separated by `;` in one call and returns one envelope per statement it ran. It is not a transaction: it stops after the first envelope whose `error` is set, and the statements before it stay committed.

```js
const envelopes = db.executeScript(
  "CREATE (:Person {name: 'Lin', role: 'engineer'}); MATCH (p:Project {name: $project}) DETACH DELETE p",
  null,
).map((text) => JSON.parse(text));
// envelopes.length       2
// envelopes[0].error     null: Lin is committed
// envelopes[1].error     "semantic error: ParameterMissing: Expected parameter(s): project"
```

[Transactions](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/api/wasm/#transactions) states the rule in the reference.

A failing statement never throws: its envelope has `error` set to a string, such as `semantic error: ParameterMissing: Expected parameter(s): name`, and the engine stays usable. Branch on `error !== null` after every call.

A call that does throw is one of these: an open or a reset that failed (the constructor, `RelateDb.open()`, `reset()`), `renderResult()` given text that is not an envelope, a call on a freed handle (`null pointer passed to rust`), or a trap. A trap is a crash inside the engine, after which that instance can't be used again. `isWasmTrap(err)` from `trap.js` tells a trap from the others:

```js
const { isWasmTrap } = await import(/* @vite-ignore */ engine('trap.js'));

try {
  self.postMessage(db.execute(statement, params));
} catch (err) {
  if (isWasmTrap(err)) self.postMessage({ trap: true });
  else throw err;
}
```

After a trap, the page terminates the worker and starts a new one. An in-memory graph is gone with it; an OPFS store reopens with every write it acknowledged.

Each statement commits on its own until `relate_conn_begin`. From then on, every statement on that connection joins one [transaction](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#transaction) until `relate_conn_commit` or `relate_conn_rollback`:

```c run name=transaction
#include <stdio.h>
#include "relate.h"

static const char *TEAM =
    "CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),"
    "       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),"
    "       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),"
    "       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),"
    "       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),"
    "       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

static int fail(const char *what, char *err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s failed: %s\n", what, err ? err : "(no detail)");
    relate_free_string(err);
    return 1;
}

/* Run a statement whose result is not needed; 0 on success. */
static int exec(relate_connection conn, const char *cypher) {
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_query_params(conn, cypher, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("query", err);
    int failed = relate_result_is_error(r);
    if (failed) fprintf(stderr, "statement failed: %s\n", relate_result_error_json(r));
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    return failed;
}

/* Run a count statement and read its one INT cell; -1 on failure. */
static long long count(relate_connection conn, const char *cypher) {
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    int64_t n = -1;
    if (relate_query_params(conn, cypher, NULL, &r, NULL) != RELATE_OK) return -1;
    relate_value_int(relate_value_at(r, 0, 0), &n);
    relate_destroy_result(r);
    return (long long)n;
}

int main(void) {
    relate_database db = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL;
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    char *bookmark = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open(":memory:", &db, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("open", err);
    if (relate_connect_namespace(db, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("connect", err);

    /* An auto-commit write: its bookmark is result-owned. */
    if (relate_query_params(conn, TEAM, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("query", err);
    printf("auto-commit bookmark: %s\n", relate_result_bookmark(r));
    relate_destroy_result(r);

    /* Commit: the bookmark comes back through an out-param, caller-owned. */
    if (relate_conn_begin(conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("begin", err);
    if (exec(conn, "MATCH (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck'}) "
                   "CREATE (:Person {name: 'Lin', role: 'engineer'})-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2026}]->(tck)"))
        return 1;
    if (relate_conn_commit(conn, &bookmark, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("commit", err);
    printf("commit bookmark: %s\n", bookmark ? bookmark : "(none)");
    relate_free_string(bookmark);
    printf("people after commit: %lld\n", count(conn, "MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p)"));

    /* Roll back: the delete is visible inside the transaction only. */
    if (relate_conn_begin(conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("begin", err);
    if (exec(conn, "MATCH (p:Project) DETACH DELETE p")) return 1;
    printf("projects inside the transaction: %lld\n", count(conn, "MATCH (p:Project) RETURN count(p)"));
    if (relate_conn_rollback(conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return fail("rollback", err);
    printf("projects after rollback: %lld\n", count(conn, "MATCH (p:Project) RETURN count(p)"));

    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(db);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
auto-commit bookmark: relate:1
commit bookmark: relate:2
people after commit: 4
projects inside the transaction: 0
projects after rollback: 2
```

A commit's [bookmark](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#bookmark), `relate:<n>`, names its place in the store's commit sequence. An auto-commit write's bookmark comes from `relate_result_bookmark(r)` and belongs to the result. An explicit transaction's comes through `relate_conn_commit`'s `bookmark_out`: a string the caller frees, or `NULL` when the transaction wrote nothing. `bookmark_out` must not be `NULL`; a call that passes `NULL` is refused and does not commit. Compare bookmarks by the parsed `n`, never as strings.

Every failure travels with the call that produced it, in one JSON shape with `code`, `gql_status`, `stage`, `retryable` and `message`. It arrives in one of two places:

- A statement that fails returns `RELATE_OK` with an error result: `relate_result_is_error(r)` is `1`, and `relate_result_error_json(r)` is the shape, owned by the result.
- Any other call that fails returns `RELATE_ERROR` and writes the shape to its trailing `char **error_json` out-param, owned by the caller and freed with `relate_free_string`.

```c run name=errors
#include <stdio.h>
#include "relate.h"

static const char *TEAM =
    "CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),"
    "       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),"
    "       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),"
    "       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),"
    "       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),"
    "       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),"
    "       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),"
    "       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

int main(void) {
    relate_database db = NULL, second = NULL;
    relate_connection conn = NULL, other = NULL;
    relate_query_result r = NULL;
    char *err = NULL;
    if (relate_database_open("./team.rl", &db, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    if (relate_connect_namespace(db, NULL, &conn, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    if (relate_query_params(conn, TEAM, NULL, &r, &err) != RELATE_OK) return 1;
    relate_destroy_result(r);

    /* 1. A statement failure: RELATE_OK, and the error rides the result (result-owned). */
    relate_status st = relate_query_params(conn,
        "MATCH (p:Person {name: $name}) RETURN p.role AS role", NULL, &r, &err);
    printf("status=%d is_error=%d\n%s\n", st, relate_result_is_error(r), relate_result_error_json(r));
    relate_destroy_result(r);

    /* 2. A call failure: RELATE_ERROR, and the error fills the out-param (caller-owned). */
    err = NULL;
    st = relate_connect_namespace(db, "people", &other, &err);
    printf("status=%d\n%s\n", st, err);
    relate_free_string(err);

    /* 3. A storage failure: the message is generic, so no path crosses the ABI. */
    err = NULL;
    st = relate_database_open("./team.rl", &second, &err);
    printf("status=%d\n%s\n", st, err);
    relate_free_string(err);

    relate_disconnect(conn);
    relate_database_close(db);
    return 0;
}
```

```text
status=0 is_error=1
{"code":"Neo.ClientError.Statement.ParameterMissing","gql_status":"42N02","stage":"compile","retryable":false,"message":"semantic error: ParameterMissing: Expected parameter(s): name"}
status=1
{"code":"Neo.ClientError.Database.DatabaseNotFound","gql_status":"42N00","stage":"compile","retryable":false,"message":"semantic error: DatabaseNotFound: Graph not found: people"}
status=1
{"code":"Neo.DatabaseError.General.UnknownError","gql_status":"42000","stage":"internal","retryable":false,"message":"internal error"}
