A decoded value binds back unchanged, so a `CypherVector` read from one statement can be a parameter of the next. A `CypherDuration` keeps months, days, seconds and nanoseconds apart, so `PT36H` and `P1DT12H` stay different values.

A connection commits each statement as its own [transaction](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#transaction) until you call `conn.begin()`. `conn.commit()` keeps the transaction and returns its [bookmark](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#bookmark), `relate:<n>`, or `null` when it wrote nothing; `conn.rollback()` discards it. An auto-commit write's bookmark is on `result.bookmark`.

```js run name=transaction
const { Database } = require("@relate/relate");

const TEAM = `
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)
`;

const conn = Database.open(":memory:").connect();
console.log("seed bookmark:", conn.query(TEAM).bookmark);

conn.begin();
conn.query(
  "MATCH (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck'}) " +
    "CREATE (:Person {name: 'Lin', role: 'engineer'})-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2026}]->(tck)"
);
conn.query("MATCH (noor:Person {name: 'Noor'}) SET noor.role = 'lead'");
console.log("commit bookmark:", conn.commit());

conn.begin();
conn.query("MATCH (p:Project) DETACH DELETE p");
conn.rollback();
console.log("projects:", conn.query("MATCH (p:Project) RETURN count(p) AS n").single().n);
```

```text
seed bookmark: relate:1
commit bookmark: relate:2
projects: 2
```

Every error the engine reports throws a `RelateError` with the engine's classification: `gqlStatus` (the GQLSTATUS code), `code` (the Neo4j status code), `stage` (`compile`, `runtime` or `internal`) and `retryable`. A parameter the client cannot convert throws a `TypeError` naming it, before anything runs.

```js run name=errors
const { Database, RelateError } = require("@relate/relate");

const TEAM = `
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)
`;

const conn = Database.open(":memory:").connect();
conn.query(TEAM);

for (const statement of [
  "MATCH (p:Person) RETURN p.name +",
  "MATCH (:Person {name: 'Ada'})-[w:WORKS_ON]->() RETURN w.since / 0",
  "MATCH (p:Person {name: $name}) RETURN p.role AS role",
]) {
  try {
    conn.query(statement);
  } catch (e) {
    if (!(e instanceof RelateError)) throw e;
    console.log(e.name, e.gqlStatus, e.stage, e.retryable);
  }
}

try {
  conn.query("MATCH (p:Person {name: $name}) RETURN p", { name: new Date(0) });
} catch (e) {
  if (!(e instanceof TypeError)) throw e;
  console.log(e.name, e.message);
}
```

```text
RelateError 42N06 compile false
RelateError 22G03 runtime false
RelateError 42N02 compile false
TypeError query params: "name" must be a plain object
```

The [errors reference](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/errors/) lists every engine message with its codes. `retryable` (also spelled `isRetriable`) is `true` only for a failure that re-running the work can fix, such as a write-write conflict, which can surface on a statement or on `commit()`. A `stage` of `internal` means the failure was outside the statement: check the store and the handle rather than retrying.

`Database.open(uri, options)` opens a store; `db.connect(namespace)` returns a connection that runs statements. The path decides the layout:

| Call                                      | Opens                                                                                 | Notes                                                                                   |
| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Database.open(":memory:")`               | An in-memory store                                                                    | Gone when the process exits                                                             |
| `Database.open("./team")`                 | A directory store                                                                     | Created when missing; another process may read it with `readOnly: true` while you write |
| `Database.open("./team.rl")`              | A [single-file store](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#single-file-store) | Created when missing; opens only while no other process writes it                       |
| `Database.open(path, { readOnly: true })` | An existing store, read-only                                                          | Loads once and refuses writes; a missing store is an error, never created               |
| `db.connect("system")`                    | A connection in another namespace                                                     | `neo4j` (default), `system` or `system_ontologies`                                      |

The client has no close method. Each statement is durable when `query()` returns, and `db.checkpoint()` writes the current state to the snapshot. A new directory store writes its snapshot with the codec in `RELATE_SNAPSHOT_FORMAT` (`parquet` by default, `json` or `markdown`). This program writes a directory store, then reads it through a second, read-only handle beside the writer:

```js run name=open
const { Database, RelateError } = require("@relate/relate");

const TEAM = `
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)
`;

const db = Database.open("./team");
db.connect().query(TEAM);
db.checkpoint();

const reader = Database.open("./team", { readOnly: true }).connect();
console.log("people:", reader.query("MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p) AS n").single().n);
try {
  reader.query("CREATE (:Person {name: 'Lin'})");
} catch (e) {
  if (!(e instanceof RelateError)) throw e;
  console.log(e.name, e.gqlStatus);
}
```

```text
people: 3
RelateError 42N18
```

A read-only handle sees the store as it was when it opened. To change namespaces later, call `conn.setNamespace(name)` between transactions, or name the target in the statement with `USE system`.

A `Result` holds every row at once in `result.rows`, plus `columns`, `stats`, `queryType`, `namespace`, `bookmark`, `notifications` and `plan`. `result.consume()` returns a `ResultSummary` with the same facts and the engine's timings. `result.graph()` collects the distinct nodes and relationships the rows carry; each has an [element id](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#element-id) (`elementId`), the string Cypher's `elementId()` returns.

```js run name=metadata
const { Database } = require("@relate/relate");

const TEAM = `
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)
`;

const conn = Database.open(":memory:").connect();
conn.query(TEAM);

const graph = conn.query("MATCH (p:Person)-[w:WORKS_ON]->(proj:Project) RETURN p, w, proj").graph();
console.log("nodes:", graph.nodes.map((n) => n.properties.name).sort().join(", "));
console.log("relationships:", graph.relationships.length);
const first = graph.nodes[0];
console.log(first.properties.name, first.elementId, first.labels.join(","));

const found = conn.query("MATCH (n) WHERE elementId(n) = $id RETURN n.name AS name", { id: first.elementId });
console.log("found by element id:", found.single().name);

const plan = conn.query("EXPLAIN MATCH (p:Person), (q:Project) RETURN p, q");
console.log("query type:", plan.queryType);
for (const n of plan.notifications) console.log(n.gql_status, n.title);
```

```text
nodes: Ada, Grace, opencypher-tck, relate
relationships: 3
Grace 4:rl-id:n1 Person
found by element id: Grace
query type: r
03N90 Cartesian product
```

`graph()` lists entities in the order the rows first carry them. A plain run leaves `notifications` empty and `plan` `null`; `EXPLAIN` and `PROFILE` fill both. `result.single()` returns the one row and throws `ResultNotSingleError` on zero rows or more than one.

`query()` blocks the event loop while the statement runs. `queryAsync()` takes the same arguments, template form included, runs the statement on a worker thread and returns a Promise of the same result. Calls on one connection run one at a time, in order; separate connections run at once.

```js run name=async
const { Database } = require("@relate/relate");

const TEAM = `
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)
`;

const TEAM_OF = "MATCH (p:Person)-[:WORKS_ON]->(:Project {name: $project}) RETURN collect(p.name) AS people";

async function main() {
  const conn = Database.open(":memory:").connect();
  await conn.queryAsync(TEAM);
  const results = await Promise.all(
    ["relate", "opencypher-tck"].map((project) => conn.queryAsync(TEAM_OF, { project }))
  );
  for (const result of results) console.log(result.single().people.sort().join(", "));
}

main().catch((e) => {
  console.error(e);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

```text
Ada, Grace
Grace
```

While an async call is in flight, a synchronous call on the same connection (`query`, `begin`, `commit`, `rollback`, `setNamespace`) throws a `RelateError` instead of waiting. A synchronous call in the same tick as `queryAsync()` runs first, because the async statement starts on the next microtask. Await the Promise first, or use a second connection.

A statement names each value it needs as `$name`. `query_with_params` takes the values as a `BTreeMap<String, Value>`, so a value never becomes part of the Cypher text:

```rust run name=params
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

use relate::{Database, Value};

const TEAM: &str = "
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

const TEAM_OF: &str = "
MATCH (p:Person)-[w:WORKS_ON]->(:Project {name: $project})
RETURN p.name AS name, w.since AS since
ORDER BY since";

fn main() -> relate::Result<()> {
    let db = Database::open(":memory:")?;
    let mut conn = db.connect()?;
    conn.query(TEAM)?;

    for project in ["relate", "opencypher-tck"] {
        let params = BTreeMap::from([("project".to_string(), Value::String(project.into()))]);
        let result = conn.query_with_params(TEAM_OF, params)?;
        println!("{project}");
        for row in &result.rows {
            if let [Value::String(name), Value::Int(since)] = row.as_slice() {
                println!("  {name} since {since}");
            }
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

```text
relate
  Grace since 2023
  Ada since 2024
opencypher-tck
  Grace since 2022
```

Each row is a `Vec<Value>` in `RETURN` order. `Value` is an enum with one variant per Cypher type (`Value::Int(i64)`, `Value::String(Arc<str>)`, `Value::Node(Arc<Node>)` and the rest), so a slice pattern reads a row's cells by type. Strings, lists, maps and vectors share their payload behind an `Arc`: build one with `.into()`, and cloning a value never copies it. The [Value mapping](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/api/rust/#value) lists every variant and its Cypher type.

`Database::open_with(uri, OpenOptions)` takes the open's settings. Of the fields this guide uses, `durability_mode`, `checkpoint_interval` and `snapshot_codec` left `None` fall back to their `RELATE_*` environment variable, then to the built-in default; `wal_dir` reads no environment variable, and `mode` is not optional. The [Rust API reference](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/api/rust/#openoptions) lists every field:

| Field                 | Default                                                                                | Sets                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `durability_mode`     | `RELATE_DURABILITY_MODE`, else `DurabilityMode::Full`                                  | When a commit is acknowledged: `Full` syncs the WAL first; `EverySecond` can lose about the last second of acknowledged writes in a crash; `Snapshot` can lose every write since the last completed checkpoint |
| `checkpoint_interval` | `RELATE_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL_SECS`, else 300 seconds                                    | The checkpoint timer; `Duration::ZERO` turns the timer off                                                                                                                                                     |
| `wal_dir`             | `<store>/wal`                                                                          | A directory store's WAL directory                                                                                                                                                                              |
| `mode`                | `OpenMode::Writable`                                                                   | `ReadOnly`, `Follow` (read-only, tailing a directory store's WAL) or `Ephemeral` (load into memory and release the source)                                                                                     |
| `snapshot_codec`      | `RELATE_SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`, else Parquet (JSON in a build without the `parquet` feature) | A new store's snapshot codec: `SnapshotCodecKind::Parquet`, `Json` or `Markdown`                                                                                                                               |

This program opens a directory store with a one-second loss window, writes a checkpoint, copies the store into a `.rl` file with `Database::clone`, and opens the copy read-only:

```rust run name=options
use std::path::Path;
use std::time::Duration;

use relate::{CloneOptions, Database, DurabilityMode, OpenMode, OpenOptions};

const TEAM: &str = "
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

fn main() -> relate::Result<()> {
    // A directory store with a one-second loss window and a 60-second timer.
    {
        let options = OpenOptions {
            durability_mode: Some(DurabilityMode::EverySecond),
            checkpoint_interval: Some(Duration::from_secs(60)),
            ..Default::default()
        };
        let db = Database::open_with("./team", options)?;
        db.connect()?.query(TEAM)?;
        db.checkpoint()?;
        println!("snapshot written: {}", db.snapshot_data_bytes()? > 0);
        println!("manifest.json: {}", Path::new("./team/manifest.json").exists());
        println!("wal/: {}", Path::new("./team/wal").is_dir());
    }

    // Copy the directory store into a single .rl file.
    Database::clone("./team", "./team.rl", CloneOptions::default())?;

    // Open the copy read-only through OpenOptions.
    let reader = Database::open_with("./team.rl", OpenOptions::with_mode(OpenMode::ReadOnly))?;
    let mut conn = reader.connect()?;
    let result = conn.query("MATCH (p:Project) RETURN count(p) AS projects")?;
    println!("read-only: {}, projects: {}", reader.is_read_only(), result.rows[0][0]);
    Ok(())
}
```

```text
snapshot written: true
manifest.json: true
wal/: true
read-only: true, projects: 2
```

`checkpoint()` writes a snapshot and recycles the closed WAL segments; it is not what makes a commit durable, which the durability mode decides. The [Rust API reference](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/api/rust/#openoptions) lists every field, including the memory limits, safe mode and the `LOAD CSV` directory.

Each `query` call commits on its own until you call `begin()`. From then on, every statement on that connection joins one [transaction](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#transaction): `commit()` publishes it as one WAL commit, and `rollback()` discards it. Other connections never see a write before its commit.

```rust run name=transaction
use relate::{Connection, Database};

const TEAM: &str = "
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

fn count(conn: &mut Connection, label: &str) -> relate::Result<String> {
    let result = conn.query(&format!("MATCH (n:{label}) RETURN count(n) AS n"))?;
    Ok(result.rows[0][0].to_string())
}

fn main() -> relate::Result<()> {
    let db = Database::open(":memory:")?;
    let mut conn = db.connect()?;
    conn.query(TEAM)?;

    // Commit: every statement since begin() lands as one WAL commit.
    conn.begin()?;
    conn.query(
        "MATCH (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck'}) \
         CREATE (:Person {name: 'Lin', role: 'engineer'})-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2026}]->(tck)",
    )?;
    let bookmark = conn.commit()?;
    println!("commit bookmark: {}", bookmark.as_deref().unwrap_or("none"));
    println!("people after commit: {}", count(&mut conn, "Person")?);

    // Roll back: the delete is visible inside the transaction only.
    conn.begin()?;
    conn.query("MATCH (p:Project) DETACH DELETE p")?;
    println!("projects inside the transaction: {}", count(&mut conn, "Project")?);
    conn.rollback()?;
    println!("projects after rollback: {}", count(&mut conn, "Project")?);

    // A transaction that writes nothing commits no bookmark.
    conn.begin()?;
    println!("empty commit: {:?}", conn.commit()?);
    Ok(())
}
```

```text
commit bookmark: relate:2
people after commit: 4
projects inside the transaction: 0
projects after rollback: 2
empty commit: None
```

`commit()` returns the transaction's [bookmark](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#bookmark), `relate:<n>`, where `n` is the store's commit sequence; a transaction that wrote nothing returns `None`. Compare bookmarks by the parsed `n`, never as strings: `relate:10` sorts before `relate:9`. A statement that fails inside a transaction fails the transaction, and only `rollback()` clears it. A second `begin()` on the same connection fails with `semantic error: already in a transaction`.

Every fallible call returns `relate::Result<T>`, and `relate::Error` is an enum whose variants are the error classes: `ParameterMissing`, `Conflict`, `Locked`, `Arithmetic` and the rest. Match the variant to branch, and call `relate::error_info(&e)` for the codes every other surface reports for the same error:

```rust run name=errors
use std::collections::BTreeMap;

use relate::{error_info, Database, Error, Value};

const TEAM: &str = "
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

const ROLE_OF: &str = "MATCH (p:Person {name: $name}) RETURN p.role AS role";

fn report(e: &Error) {
    let info = error_info(e);
    println!("{e}");
    println!("  code={} gql_status={} stage={:?} retryable={}",
        info.neo4j_code, info.gql_status, info.stage, info.retryable);
}

fn main() -> relate::Result<()> {
    let db = Database::open("./team.rl")?;
    let mut conn = db.connect()?;
    conn.query(TEAM)?;

    // A statement that names $name without a value fails before it runs.
    match conn.query(ROLE_OF) {
        Err(e @ Error::ParameterMissing(_)) => report(&e),
        other => println!("unexpected: {:?}", other.map(|r| r.rows)),
    }

    // A second writer on the same store is refused while this one is open.
    match Database::open("./team.rl") {
        Err(e @ Error::Locked(_)) => report(&e),
        other => println!("unexpected: {}", other.is_ok()),
    }

    // The connection stays usable after an error.
    let params = BTreeMap::from([("name".to_string(), Value::String("Noor".into()))]);
    let result = conn.query_with_params(ROLE_OF, params)?;
    if let [Value::String(role)] = result.rows[0].as_slice() {
        println!("Noor is a {role}");
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

```text
semantic error: ParameterMissing: Expected parameter(s): name
  code=Neo.ClientError.Statement.ParameterMissing gql_status=42N02 stage=Compile retryable=false
storage locked: ./team.rl
  code=Neo.DatabaseError.General.UnknownError gql_status=42000 stage=Internal retryable=false
Noor is a maintainer
```

`error_info` returns the Neo4j status code, the GQLSTATUS code, the stage (`Compile` before any work, `Runtime` while producing rows, `Internal` when the statement is not at fault), and `retryable`, which is `true` only for a write-write conflict (`Error::Conflict`) and a retryable processing limit. The engine never retries on its own. Outside an explicit transaction, the connection stays usable after a statement error.

`Database::open` takes the store's location and creates the store when nothing is there yet:

| Argument               | Store                                                                                                               |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `":memory:"`           | An in-memory store, gone when the `Database` is dropped                                                             |
| A path ending in `.rl` | A [single-file store](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#single-file-store): snapshot and WAL in one file |
| Any other path         | A directory store: snapshot files beside a `wal/` directory                                                         |

A `file://` URI names a path the same way. `db.connect()` returns a `Connection`, and `query` runs one statement and returns a `QueryResult`. This program writes the team graph to an in-memory store and to `team.rl`, then reopens the file with `Database::open_read_only`:

```rust run name=open
use relate::Database;

const TEAM: &str = "
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

fn main() -> relate::Result<()> {
    // An in-memory store: gone when the Database is dropped.
    let memory = Database::open(":memory:")?;
    let mut conn = memory.connect()?;
    let created = conn.query(TEAM)?;
    println!("in memory: {} nodes, {} relationships",
        created.stats.nodes_created, created.stats.relationships_created);

    // A single-file store: created on first open, durable after each commit.
    {
        let file = Database::open("./team.rl")?;
        file.connect()?.query(TEAM)?;
    }

    // Reopen the same file read-only: reads work, writes fail.
    let reader = Database::open_read_only("./team.rl")?;
    let mut conn = reader.connect()?;
    let people = conn.query("MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p) AS people")?;
    println!("team.rl: {} people", people.rows[0][0]);
    println!("write refused: {}", conn.query("CREATE (:Person {name: 'Lin'})").is_err());
    Ok(())
}
```

```text
in memory: 5 nodes, 5 relationships
team.rl: 3 people
write refused: true
```

A `Database` is `Send` and `Sync`, so threads can share one and each open its own `Connection`. A `Connection` is `Send` but not `Sync`: its methods take `&mut self`, one statement at a time. A read-only open never creates, writes, or checkpoints the store, and a write on it fails with `WriteOnReadOnlyAccessDatabase`.

`EXPLAIN` plans a statement without running it, and `PROFILE` runs it and stamps each operator with what it did. Either prefix gives the result a plan tree, at `plan_root()`, and the [notifications](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#notification) derived with it, at `notifications()`. A plain run carries neither.

```rust run name=plan
use relate::{Database, PlanNode};

const TEAM: &str = "
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

fn print_tree(node: &PlanNode, depth: usize) {
    let rows = node.metrics.as_ref().map(|m| m.rows.to_string()).unwrap_or("-".into());
    println!("{}{} [{}] rows={}", "  ".repeat(depth), node.operator, node.details, rows);
    for child in &node.children {
        print_tree(child, depth + 1);
    }
}

fn main() -> relate::Result<()> {
    let db = Database::open(":memory:")?;
    let mut conn = db.connect()?;
    conn.query(TEAM)?;

    // EXPLAIN plans without running and derives the notifications.
    let explained = conn.query("EXPLAIN MATCH (p:Person), (q:Project) RETURN p.name, q.name")?;
    for n in explained.notifications() {
        println!("{} {} {}: {}", n.gql_status(), n.severity().as_str(), n.title(), n.description());
    }

    // PROFILE runs the statement and stamps rows on each operator.
    let profiled = conn.query(
        "PROFILE MATCH (p:Person)-[:WORKS_ON]->(:Project {name: 'relate'}) RETURN p.name AS name",
    )?;
    if let Some(root) = profiled.plan_root() {
        print_tree(root, 0);
    }
    println!("rows returned: {}", profiled.rows.len());
    Ok(())
}
```

```text
03N90 INFORMATION Cartesian product: the MATCH patterns share no join anchor
ProduceResults [1 item(s)] rows=2
  Expand [(:Project)<-[:WORKS_ON]-(p:Person)] rows=2
rows returned: 2
```

Each `PlanNode` has an `operator`, `details`, `estimated_rows`, `children`, typed `warnings` and, under `PROFILE`, `metrics` with rows, database hits, memory, and time. `Connection::explain_plan(cypher)` returns the same tree for a statement without running it or binding parameters. The [notifications reference](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/cypher/notifications/) lists each code and its remedy.

A `QueryResult` carries its rows and a `header` describing them, the statement's counters in `stats`, and the auto-commit write's bookmark in `bookmark`:

```rust run name=metadata
use relate::Database;

const TEAM: &str = "
CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
       (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
       (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
       (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
       (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
       (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
       (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
       (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
       (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)";

fn main() -> relate::Result<()> {
    let db = Database::open(":memory:")?;
    let mut conn = db.connect()?;
    let created = conn.query(TEAM)?;
    println!("bookmark: {:?}", created.bookmark);

    let result = conn.query(
        "MATCH (p:Person)-[w:WORKS_ON]->(:Project {name: 'relate'}) \
         RETURN p.name AS name, w.since AS since ORDER BY since",
    )?;
    println!("columns: {:?}", result.header.columns);
    println!("column types: {:?}", result.header.column_types);
    println!("query type: {}", result.header.query_type.as_str());
    println!("namespace: {:?}", result.header.namespace);
    println!("bookmark: {:?}", result.bookmark);

    let write = conn.query("MATCH (n:Person {name: 'Noor'}) SET n.role = 'lead'")?;
    println!("query type: {}", write.header.query_type.as_str());
    println!("properties set: {}", write.stats.properties_set);
    Ok(())
}
```

```text
bookmark: Some("relate:1")
columns: ["name", "since"]
column types: ["STRING", "INTEGER"]
query type: r
namespace: Neo4j
bookmark: None
query type: w
properties set: 1
```

`column_types` names each column's type from the first row; a column whose first value is null, or a result with no rows, reports `ANY`. `query_type` comes from the plan before the statement runs: `r` for a read, `w` for a write with no returned columns, `rw` for a write that returns columns, and `s` for schema commands. `elapsed_ms()` and its split into `parse_ms()`, `plan_ms()` and `execute_ms()` are the engine's own timings for the statement.

Build and run your program with `cargo run`. A program that opens a store, writes the team graph and reads it back prints the counts it wrote. Reading a persistent store back from a second program is the end-to-end check, so open it read-only:

