Outside a transaction, each `query` commits on its own. `conn.transaction { … }` runs its body in an explicit [transaction](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#transaction), commits when the body returns and rolls back, then rethrows, when it throws. `begin()`, `commit()` and `rollback()` do the same by hand, and `commit()` returns the [bookmark](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#bookmark), `"relate:<n>"`, or `nil` when the transaction wrote nothing. When another connection commits a change to the same node first, the commit throws an `.engine` error whose `retryable` is true: re-run the whole unit of work. This program commits one change, rolls one back, and retries a unit of work that loses a conflict on its first attempt:

```swift run name=transaction
import Relate

let team = """
    CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
           (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
           (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
           (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
           (tck:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck', language: 'Gherkin'}),
           (ada)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2024}]->(relate),
           (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2023}]->(relate),
           (grace)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2022}]->(tck),
           (noor)-[:MAINTAINS]->(relate),
           (ada)-[:KNOWS]->(grace)
    """

func withRetry(_ conn: Connection, _ body: (Connection, Int) throws -> Void) throws {
    var attempt = 1
    while true {
        do {
            return try conn.transaction { try body($0, attempt) }
        } catch let error as RelateError where error.engineError?.retryable == true && attempt < 3 {
            print("attempt \(attempt): \(error.message)")
            attempt += 1
        }
    }
}

let db = try Database.inMemory()
let conn = try db.connect()
let other = try db.connect()
try conn.query(team)

try conn.begin()
try conn.query("""
    MATCH (n:Person {name: 'Noor'}), (t:Project {name: 'opencypher-tck'})
    CREATE (n)-[:WORKS_ON {since: 2025}]->(t)
    """)
try conn.query("MATCH (n:Person {name: 'Noor'}) SET n.role = 'engineer'")
print("committed:", try conn.commit() ?? "nothing")

try conn.begin()
try conn.query("MATCH (a:Person {name: 'Ada'}) DETACH DELETE a")
try conn.rollback()
print("people:", try conn.query("MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p)").scalar())

try withRetry(conn) { tx, attempt in
    try tx.query("MATCH (p:Project {name: 'relate'}) SET p.reviews = coalesce(p.reviews, 0) + 1")
    if attempt == 1 {
        try other.query("MATCH (p:Project {name: 'relate'}) SET p.reviews = 10")
    }
}
print("reviews:", try conn.query("MATCH (p:Project {name: 'relate'}) RETURN p.reviews").scalar())
```

```text
committed: relate:2
people: 3
attempt 1: write-write conflict
reviews: 11
```

Compare bookmarks by their number, never as strings: `"relate:10"` sorts before `"relate:9"`. An auto-commit write carries its bookmark in `result.bookmark`. [Transactions and concurrency](https://relatedb.ai/docs/concepts/transactions-and-concurrency/) explains what a transaction isolates.

Add the agent to `graph-tool.mjs` in place of the check block. Mastra resolves the model id `anthropic/claude-opus-5-5` through its model router, which reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`; without the key, `generate` throws `Could not find API key process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY for model id anthropic/claude-opus-5-5`.

```js
import { Agent } from "@mastra/core/agent";

const analyst = new Agent({
  id: "team-analyst",
  name: "Team analyst",
  instructions: "Answer questions about the team graph. Use the read-graph tool for every fact, and pass values as params.",
  model: "anthropic/claude-opus-5-5",
  tools: { readGraph },
});

const result = await analyst.generate("Who works on the relate project, and since when?", { maxSteps: 5 });
console.log(result.text);
```

`maxSteps: 5` lets the model call the tool, read the rows and answer, in at most five steps. To keep a person in the loop on a tool that writes, Mastra's `requireApproval: true` on `createTool` pauses each call until it is approved; the read-only handle stays the guard that the engine enforces.

The program has three parts. A setup block writes the team graph the Get started pages use into `./team`, a directory store, standing in for the app that owns the data. The tool, `readGraph`, is a Mastra `createTool` with its own read-only handle on that store. The last block calls the tool's `execute` directly, the way an agent's tool call reaches it, with no model, and then tries a write on the read-only handle itself:

```js run name=graph-tool title="graph-tool.mjs"
import { Database, RelateError } from "@relate/relate";
import { createTool } from "@mastra/core/tools";
import { z } from "zod";

// Setup: the app that owns ./team writes the team graph once, on a writable handle.
const owner = Database.open("./team").connect();
if (owner.query("MATCH (p:Person) RETURN count(p) AS n").rows[0].n === 0) {
  owner.query(`
    CREATE (ada:Person {name: 'Ada', role: 'engineer'}),
           (grace:Person {name: 'Grace', role: 'engineer'}),
           (noor:Person {name: 'Noor', role: 'maintainer'}),
           (relate:Project {name: 'relate', language: 'Rust'}),
