`RELATE_MAX_ROWS=1` caps every statement at one row. `--max-rows 0` on the same command beats it and prints every row:

```bash verify
RELATE_MAX_ROWS=1 relate --mode jsonl "UNWIND [1, 2, 3] AS x RETURN x"
RELATE_MAX_ROWS=1 relate --mode jsonl --max-rows 0 "UNWIND [1, 2, 3] AS x RETURN x"
```

```text
warning: result truncated to 1 rows (the statement returned 3); pass --max-rows 0 for all rows
{"x":1}
{"rowCap":{"kept":1,"total":3}}
{"x":1}
{"x":2}
{"x":3}
```

The same holds for every pair: `--no-safe` beats `RELATE_SAFE_MODE=1`, `--durability-mode` beats `RELATE_DURABILITY_MODE`, and `--load-csv-dir` beats `RELATE_LOAD_CSV_DIR`.

The Node.js client passes [parameters](https://relatedb.ai/docs/reference/glossary/#parameter) to the engine through a bounded builder:

| Limit                           | Value                     | Met as                                                                                |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Entries in one list or map      | 100,000                   | `parameter container exceeds the entry limit of 100000`                               |
| Length of one parameter name    | 4,096 bytes               | `parameter name exceeds the 4096-byte limit`                                          |
| All parameters of one statement | 16 MiB (16,777,216 bytes) | `string parameter exceeds the builder's total byte budget of 16777216` (for a string) |

This program trips each one:

```js run name=node-parameter-limits title="limits.cjs"
const { Database } = require("@relate/relate");

const conn = Database.open(":memory:").connect();
const cases = {
  "100,001 list entries": { xs: Array.from({ length: 100001 }, (_, i) => i) },
  "a 4,097-byte name": { ["n".repeat(4097)]: 1 },
  "a 17 MiB string": { s: "a".repeat(17 * 1024 * 1024) },
};
for (const [what, params] of Object.entries(cases)) {
  try {
    conn.query("RETURN 1 AS ok", params);
    console.log(`${what}: accepted`);
  } catch (e) {
    console.log(`${what}: ${e.message}`);
  }
}
```

```text
100,001 list entries: parameter container exceeds the entry limit of 100000
a 4,097-byte name: parameter name exceeds the 4096-byte limit
a 17 MiB string: string parameter exceeds the builder's total byte budget of 16777216
```

